al scale. Then say when you would switch."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000009", "q": "How do I describe a CI/CD pipeline?", "tags": ["technical", "cicd", "pipeline", "devops", "deployment"], "a": "Walk the path from commit to production: lint and unit tests on pull request, integration tests on merge, staging deploy, smoke tests, then production with canary or blue-green deployment. Name the rollback plan, because every pipeline needs one. Mention deploy dashboards and alerts; a pipeline without monitoring is a hope, not a process."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000010", "q": "How should I talk about technical debt?", "tags": ["technical", "technical debt", "legacy"], "a": "Show triage thinking: distinguish debt that charges interest on every change from debt sitting quietly in stable corners. Schedule high-interest payoff like a feature, and translate the cost into velocity so product understands the budget ask. Document and defer the low-interest kind. Never demand a full rewrite and never ignore debt entirely."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000011", "q": "How do I answer situational questions about tight deadlines?", "tags": ["situational", "deadline", "pressure", "changing requirements"], "a": "Re-anchor on the real goal, renegotiate scope explicitly, and get sign-off on the tradeoff instead of silently crunching. Propose what can shrink, execute visibly with short check-ins, and treat heroics as a last resort, not a plan. Silent overtime as the default answer predicts burnout, not delivery."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000012", "q": "What if I disagree with my manager in a scenario question?", "tags": ["situational", "disagree", "manager", "dissent"], "a": "Raise it privately with evidence and alternatives, and listen fully because you might be wrong. Once a decision is made, commit publicly even if you lost the argument: disagree and commit. Never go over their head and never sulk afterward. The interviewer is checking both courage and loyalty."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000013", "q": "How do I s