al scale. Then say when you would switch."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000009", "q": "How do I describe a CI/CD pipeline?", "tags": ["technical", "cicd", "pipeline", "devops", "deployment"], "a": "Walk the path from commit to production: lint and unit tests on pull request, integration tests on merge, staging deploy, smoke tests, then production with canary or blue-green deployment. Name the rollback plan, because every pipeline needs one. Mention deploy dashboards and alerts; a pipeline without monitoring is a hope, not a process."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000010", "q": "How should I talk about technical debt?", "tags": ["technical", "technical debt", "legacy"], "a": "Show triage thinking: distinguish debt that charges interest on every change from debt sitting quietly in stable corners. Schedule high-interest payoff like a feature, and translate the cost into velocity so product understands the budget ask. Document and defer the low-interest kind. Never demand a full rewrite and never ignore debt entirely."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000011", "q": "How do I answer situational questions about tight deadlines?", "tags": ["situational", "deadline", "pressure", "changing requirements"], "a": "Re-anchor on the real goal, renegotiate scope explicitly, and get sign-off on the tradeoff instead of silently crunching. Propose what can shrink, execute visibly with short check-ins, and treat heroics as a last resort, not a plan. Silent overtime as the default answer predicts burnout, not delivery."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000012", "q": "What if I disagree with my manager in a scenario question?", "tags": ["situational", "disagree", "manager", "dissent"], "a": "Raise it privately with evidence and alternatives, and listen fully because you might be wrong. Once a decision is made, commit publicly even if you lost the argument: disagree and commit. Never go over their head and never sulk afterward. The interviewer is checking both courage and loyalty."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000013", "q": "How do I show I can learn new technology quickly?", "tags": ["situational", "learning", "new technology", "ramp up"], "a": "Describe your learning system with a real example: build a small real project in week one, read the canonical guide, learn the community idioms, and get code reviewed by someone experienced. Set time-boxed milestones. A method with proof beats claiming you just pick things up, which sounds like luck."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000014", "q": "How do I answer the angry customer scenario?", "tags": ["situational", "angry customer", "support", "de-escalation"], "a": "Listen fully before responding, acknowledge the emotion, and repeat the issue back to prove you heard it. Apologize for the experience, then move to concrete next steps with a timeline, and follow up afterward. Never argue facts while someone is emotional: regulation before resolution."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000015", "q": "How do I demonstrate leading without authority?", "tags": ["leadership", "lead", "influence", "without authority"], "a": "Show the mechanics of influence: writing the clear proposal, doing the early legwork yourself, finding stakeholders' real incentives, and making the decision easy. Influence is built before it is spent. Give an example where people followed because the idea was good and the work was shared, not because of a title."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000016", "q": "What is the best structure for giving difficult feedback?", "tags": ["leadership", "feedback", "difficult conversation", "management"], "a": "Use SBI: situation, behavior, impact, then the request. Be specific and prompt, always in private, and pair the feedback with a path forward plus follow-up. It is a conversation starter, not a verdict. Never sandwich praise around criticism to soften it and never delay hard conversations."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000017", "q": "How do I answer prioritization questions as a leader?", "tags": ["leadership", "prioritization", "competing projects", "strategy"], "a": "Apply the framework openly: impact, effort, urgency, and strategic fit. Make tradeoffs explicit, name what is being deprioritized and why, and revisit on a cadence. Stakeholders accept no when they understand the yes. Never claim you would do everything; that predicts nothing delivered well."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000018", "q": "How do I handle the salary expectation question?", "tags": ["compensation", "salary", "negotiation", "pay"], "a": "Give a researched range, not a single number, anchored to market data for the role, level, and location. State the range confidently and let them position within it. Deflecting forever frustrates; answering with a range shows preparation. Keep the tone collaborative: you are solving the fit together, not haggling."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000019", "q": "What questions should I ask the interviewer?", "tags": ["questions to ask", "interviewer", "reverse interview"], "a": "Ask about the actual work: what does success look like in six months, what is the hardest problem the team faces, and how are decisions made here. Ask your future manager what they value in top performers. Avoid questions answered by the website. Good questions prove you are evaluating them too, which strong candidates do."}, {"qid": "JAH-INTAI-000020", "q": "How do I follow up after the interview?", "tags": ["follow up", "thank you", "after interview"], "a": "Send a brief thank-you within twenty-four hours: one specific thing you discussed, one reason you are excited about the role, and no groveling. If they gave a timeline, respect it; if it passes, one polite nudge is fine. Keep every follow-up short. The interview is the product; the follow-up is the receipt."}];
const STOP = new Set(["a", "about", "after", "again", "all", "am", "an", "and", "any", "are", "as", "at", "be", "before", "between", "both", "by", "can", "could", "do", "down", "each", "few", "for", "from", "had", "has", "have", "here", "how", "i", "if", "in", "into", "is", "it", "just", "me", "more", "most", "my", "now", "of", "off", "on", "once", "only", "or", "other", "out", "over", "own", "s", "same", "should", "so", "some", "such", "t", "than", "that", "the", "their", "them", "then", "there", "these", "this", "those", "to", "too", "up", "very", "vs", "was", "were", "what", "when", "where", "which", "who", "whom", "will", "with", "would", "you", "your"]);
function tokens(s){
  return s.toLowerCase().split(/[^a-z0-9]+/).filter(function(w){return w.length>1 && !STOP.has(w);});
}
function answer(query){
